---
title: Overview
id: interrupts-overview
order: 1
description: "Pause an agent run for a human or application decision, then continue it exactly where it stopped."
keywords:
  - tanstack ai
  - ag-ui interrupts
  - human in the loop
  - tool approval
  - resolveInterrupt
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Most agent runs are fire and forget. The model calls tools, they run, you get an\nanswer back. But some steps must not happen on their own: moving money,\ndeleting a project, sending an email. And sometimes the agent needs an answer\nonly the user can give before it can go on."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An interrupt is a pause. The run stops, hands you a decision to make, and then\npicks up exactly where it left off once you answer."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-it-works","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How it works"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The server reaches a step that needs a decision and ends the run with an\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupt"},{"type":"text","value":" outcome instead of a final answer."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The client gives you the pending decisions as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You resolve each one (approve, reject, submit a value, or cancel)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The client starts a fresh continuation run that carries your answers and\ncontinues the agent."}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant User\n    participant Client\n    participant Server\n\n    Client->>Server: send message, run starts\n    Server-->>Client: interrupt outcome, run ends without a final answer\n    Client->>User: pending decisions surface as `interrupts`\n    User->>Client: approve / reject / submit a value\n    Client->>Server: continuation request with the answers, a fresh run\n    Server-->>Client: the agent picks up where it paused, final answer","lang":"mermaid"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note that the pause spans "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"two runs"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the interrupted one ends, and the\ncontinuation is a new run. One user-visible turn, two run lifecycles. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../chat/stream-events#threads-and-runs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Threads and runs"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When replaying saved events, the client follows "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parentRunId"},{"type":"text","value":" links to avoid\nshowing a resolved pause again. A continuation that ends without an interrupt\nclears its ancestor's pending decisions, even if the parent link arrives later.\nIntermediate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tool_calls"},{"type":"text","value":" events do not mark the run or its ancestors as answered."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No database is required. The browser sends the full message history back on the\ncontinuation request, so a stateless server can rebuild the paused step and keep\ngoing."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"what-pauses-a-run","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What pauses a run"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two kinds of interrupt show up in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts"},{"type":"text","value":" array for you to resolve:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kind"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You get a pause when"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Guide"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tool-approval"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A tool is marked "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval"},{"type":"text","value":" and the model calls it"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./tool-approval","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Approval"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generic"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Middleware requests typed client data at a lifecycle boundary"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./generic","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generic Interrupts"}]}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An MCP server can also ask for input during a tool call. The run ends with an interrupt. The page is "},{"type":"link","href":"../tools/mcp-input","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP client input"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"first-party-generic-interrupts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"First-party generic interrupts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a generic interrupt that TanStack AI owns, define it once with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineInterrupt()"},{"type":"text","value":". Register the definition with both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat({ interrupts })"},{"type":"text","value":"\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat({ interrupts })"},{"type":"text","value":". Middleware emits it through\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onInterruptBoundary"},{"type":"text","value":", and the client receives a typed bound item that it can\nresolve or cancel. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./generic","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generic Interrupts"}]},{"type":"text","value":". To pick a phase, see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./boundaries","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lifecycle Boundaries"}]},{"type":"text","value":". To apply the answer, see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./apply-answers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Apply Answers"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"external-generic-interrupts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"External generic interrupts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An interrupt is a standard AG-UI object, and TanStack AI is not the only thing\nthat can put one on a stream. A workflow engine pausing for a durable approval,\nor another agent framework sharing the same connection, emits the same envelope."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are three cases:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A registered first-party generic interrupt has "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kind: 'generic'"},{"type":"text","value":", a literal\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"definitionId"},{"type":"text","value":", typed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"payload"},{"type":"text","value":", and a typed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveInterrupt"},{"type":"text","value":" method."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An external generic interrupt with a valid binding also has "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kind: 'generic'"},{"type":"text","value":".\nIts response is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":", but it has "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveInterrupt"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancel"},{"type":"text","value":", and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clearResolution"},{"type":"text","value":". It joins the root batch controls."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An interrupt with a missing, malformed, or unsupported binding has\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kind: 'unbound'"},{"type":"text","value":". It remains visible, but has no controls."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The binding is stored in the interrupt metadata under\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"INTERRUPT_BINDING_METADATA_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":". It records the interrupted run and generation.\nThe client uses it to send the answer to the matching paused step."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Render unbound items as status information. Do not render a response form for\nthem:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst transferTool = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'transfer',\n  description: 'Move money between accounts',\n  needsApproval: true,\n  inputSchema: z.object({ recipient: z.string(), amount: z.number() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ receiptId: z.string() }),\n}).client()\n\nexport function Pauses() {\n  const { interrupts } = useChat({\n    threadId: 'thread-1',\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    tools: [transferTool] as const,\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      {interrupts.map((interrupt) => {\n        if (interrupt.kind === 'unbound') {\n          return (\n            <p key={interrupt.id}>\n              External pause: {interrupt.message ?? interrupt.reason}\n            </p>\n          )\n        }\n        if (interrupt.kind === 'generic') {\n          return (\n            <article key={interrupt.id}>\n              <p>{interrupt.message ?? interrupt.reason}</p>\n              <button\n                onClick={() =>\n                  interrupt.resolveInterrupt({ speed: 'express' })\n                }\n              >\n                Choose express\n              </button>\n              <button onClick={() => interrupt.cancel()}>Cancel</button>\n              <button onClick={() => interrupt.clearResolution()}>\n                Clear choice\n              </button>\n            </article>\n          )\n        }\n        return (\n          <button\n            key={interrupt.id}\n            onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}\n          >\n            Approve {interrupt.toolName}\n          </button>\n        )\n      })}\n    </>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The library will not invent a binding to make these resolvable. Doing so would\nrender a form whose answer is sent to a run that has nothing pending. The\nsubmit would fail only after the user has filled it in. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unbound"},{"type":"text","value":" says that\nthe pause belongs to something else. Unbound items never block you from\nresolving the ones that are yours."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If an external producer wants the chat client to resume its pause, attach a\nvalid binding with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withInterruptBinding"},{"type":"text","value":". Do not write the metadata key by\nhand. Use the exact interrupted run id and generation that own the pause:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  INTERRUPT_BINDING_VERSION,\n  canonicalInterruptJson,\n  digestInterruptJson,\n  withInterruptBinding,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nconst responseSchema = {\n  type: 'object',\n  properties: { speed: { type: 'string' } },\n  required: ['speed'],\n}\n\nconst descriptor = withInterruptBinding(\n  {\n    id: 'shipping-1',\n    reason: 'confirmation',\n    message: 'Which shipping speed?',\n    responseSchema,\n  },\n  {\n    v: INTERRUPT_BINDING_VERSION,\n    kind: 'generic',\n    interruptId: 'shipping-1',\n    interruptedRunId: 'run-42',\n    generation: 0,\n    // The server checks the schema it hands out still matches the one it\n    // validates against, so the hash is computed from the schema itself.\n    responseSchemaHash: digestInterruptJson(\n      canonicalInterruptJson(responseSchema),\n    ),\n  },\n)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The client treats this as an untyped generic interrupt. The example above can\nstage a value, cancel it, or clear the draft. It can also join\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveInterrupts(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" with tool approvals and first-party generic interrupts."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"v"},{"type":"text","value":" is the binding wire version. The client rejects unknown versions and bad\nfields. Those interrupts become "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unbound"},{"type":"text","value":" rather than a form that cannot\nresume the owner."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"what-about-client-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What about client tools?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A tool with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client()"},{"type":"text","value":" implementation runs in the browser on its own and\nreports its own result. That is not a decision you make, so it never appears in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts"},{"type":"text","value":". See "},{"type":"link","href":"../tools/client-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If persistence restores a pending client-tool execution, the client leaves it\npending rather than running the browser code again. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../persistence/client-persistence#handle-restored-client-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor recovery policies."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The one time a tool pauses is when you mark it "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval: true"},{"type":"text","value":". Then it\nstops for a yes or no first, whether it runs on the server or in the browser:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What you handle"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server tool"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nothing, unless "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval"},{"type":"text","value":" adds a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tool-approval"},{"type":"text","value":" pause. It then runs on the server after you approve."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client tool"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nothing, it runs in the browser automatically. With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval"},{"type":"text","value":" it pauses for approval first, then runs in the browser."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"So approval is the only thing you resolve for either kind of tool, and both use\nthe same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tool-approval"},{"type":"text","value":" interrupt."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"where-to-go-next","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where to go next"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want to"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Page"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Approve or reject a single tool call"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./tool-approval","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Approval"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resolve several pending decisions at once"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./multiple","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multiple Interrupts"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ask the user something that isn't a tool"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./generic","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generic Interrupts"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read an MCP input request"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../tools/mcp-input","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP client input"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pick "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"beforeModel"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"afterModel"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"beforeTools"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"afterTools"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./boundaries","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lifecycle Boundaries"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Apply a generic answer to prompts or stop the run"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./apply-answers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Apply Answers"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Run a tool in the browser"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../tools/client-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client Tools"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Move off the old "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"approval-requested"},{"type":"text","value":" events"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./migration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Migration"}]}]}]]}],"headings":[{"id":"how-it-works","text":"How it works","level":2},{"id":"what-pauses-a-run","text":"What pauses a run","level":2},{"id":"first-party-generic-interrupts","text":"First-party generic interrupts","level":2},{"id":"external-generic-interrupts","text":"External generic interrupts","level":2},{"id":"what-about-client-tools","text":"What about client tools?","level":2},{"id":"where-to-go-next","text":"Where to go next","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nMost agent runs are fire and forget. The model calls tools, they run, you get an\nanswer back. But some steps must not happen on their own: moving money,\ndeleting a project, sending an email. And sometimes the agent needs an answer\nonly the user can give before it can go on.\n\nAn interrupt is a pause. The run stops, hands you a decision to make, and then\npicks up exactly where it left off once you answer.\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The server reaches a step that needs a decision and ends the run with an\n   `interrupt` outcome instead of a final answer.\n2. The client gives you the pending decisions as `interrupts`.\n3. You resolve each one (approve, reject, submit a value, or cancel).\n4. The client starts a fresh continuation run that carries your answers and\n   continues the agent.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant User\n    participant Client\n    participant Server\n\n    Client->>Server: send message, run starts\n    Server-->>Client: interrupt outcome, run ends without a final answer\n    Client->>User: pending decisions surface as `interrupts`\n    User->>Client: approve / reject / submit a value\n    Client->>Server: continuation request with the answers, a fresh run\n    Server-->>Client: the agent picks up where it paused, final answer\n```\n\nNote that the pause spans **two runs**: the interrupted one ends, and the\ncontinuation is a new run. One user-visible turn, two run lifecycles. See\n[Threads and runs](../chat/stream-events#threads-and-runs).\n\nWhen replaying saved events, the client follows `parentRunId` links to avoid\nshowing a resolved pause again. A continuation that ends without an interrupt\nclears its ancestor's pending decisions, even if the parent link arrives later.\nIntermediate `tool_calls` events do not mark the run or its ancestors as answered.\n\nNo database is required. The browser sends the full message history back on the\ncontinuation request, so a stateless server can rebuild the paused step and keep\ngoing.\n\n## What pauses a run\n\nTwo kinds of interrupt show up in the `interrupts` array for you to resolve:\n\n| `kind` | You get a pause when | Guide |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `tool-approval` | A tool is marked `needsApproval` and the model calls it | [Tool Approval](./tool-approval) |\n| `generic` | Middleware requests typed client data at a lifecycle boundary | [Generic Interrupts](./generic) |\n\nAn MCP server can also ask for input during a tool call. The run ends with an interrupt. The page is [MCP client input](../tools/mcp-input).\n\n## First-party generic interrupts\n\nFor a generic interrupt that TanStack AI owns, define it once with\n`defineInterrupt()`. Register the definition with both `chat({ interrupts })`\nand `useChat({ interrupts })`. Middleware emits it through\n`onInterruptBoundary`, and the client receives a typed bound item that it can\nresolve or cancel. See [Generic Interrupts](./generic). To pick a phase, see\n[Lifecycle Boundaries](./boundaries). To apply the answer, see\n[Apply Answers](./apply-answers).\n\n## External generic interrupts\n\nAn interrupt is a standard AG-UI object, and TanStack AI is not the only thing\nthat can put one on a stream. A workflow engine pausing for a durable approval,\nor another agent framework sharing the same connection, emits the same envelope.\n\nThere are three cases:\n\n- A registered first-party generic interrupt has `kind: 'generic'`, a literal\n  `definitionId`, typed `payload`, and a typed `resolveInterrupt` method.\n- An external generic interrupt with a valid binding also has `kind: 'generic'`.\n  Its response is `unknown`, but it has `resolveInterrupt`, `cancel`, and\n  `clearResolution`. It joins the root batch controls.\n- An interrupt with a missing, malformed, or unsupported binding has\n  `kind: 'unbound'`. It remains visible, but has no controls.\n\nThe binding is stored in the interrupt metadata under\n`INTERRUPT_BINDING_METADATA_KEY`. It records the interrupted run and generation.\nThe client uses it to send the answer to the matching paused step.\n\nRender unbound items as status information. Do not render a response form for\nthem:\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst transferTool = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'transfer',\n  description: 'Move money between accounts',\n  needsApproval: true,\n  inputSchema: z.object({ recipient: z.string(), amount: z.number() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ receiptId: z.string() }),\n}).client()\n\nexport function Pauses() {\n  const { interrupts } = useChat({\n    threadId: 'thread-1',\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    tools: [transferTool] as const,\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      {interrupts.map((interrupt) => {\n        if (interrupt.kind === 'unbound') {\n          return (\n            <p key={interrupt.id}>\n              External pause: {interrupt.message ?? interrupt.reason}\n            </p>\n          )\n        }\n        if (interrupt.kind === 'generic') {\n          return (\n            <article key={interrupt.id}>\n              <p>{interrupt.message ?? interrupt.reason}</p>\n              <button\n                onClick={() =>\n                  interrupt.resolveInterrupt({ speed: 'express' })\n                }\n              >\n                Choose express\n              </button>\n              <button onClick={() => interrupt.cancel()}>Cancel</button>\n              <button onClick={() => interrupt.clearResolution()}>\n                Clear choice\n              </button>\n            </article>\n          )\n        }\n        return (\n          <button\n            key={interrupt.id}\n            onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}\n          >\n            Approve {interrupt.toolName}\n          </button>\n        )\n      })}\n    </>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe library will not invent a binding to make these resolvable. Doing so would\nrender a form whose answer is sent to a run that has nothing pending. The\nsubmit would fail only after the user has filled it in. `unbound` says that\nthe pause belongs to something else. Unbound items never block you from\nresolving the ones that are yours.\n\nIf an external producer wants the chat client to resume its pause, attach a\nvalid binding with `withInterruptBinding`. Do not write the metadata key by\nhand. Use the exact interrupted run id and generation that own the pause:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  INTERRUPT_BINDING_VERSION,\n  canonicalInterruptJson,\n  digestInterruptJson,\n  withInterruptBinding,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nconst responseSchema = {\n  type: 'object',\n  properties: { speed: { type: 'string' } },\n  required: ['speed'],\n}\n\nconst descriptor = withInterruptBinding(\n  {\n    id: 'shipping-1',\n    reason: 'confirmation',\n    message: 'Which shipping speed?',\n    responseSchema,\n  },\n  {\n    v: INTERRUPT_BINDING_VERSION,\n    kind: 'generic',\n    interruptId: 'shipping-1',\n    interruptedRunId: 'run-42',\n    generation: 0,\n    // The server checks the schema it hands out still matches the one it\n    // validates against, so the hash is computed from the schema itself.\n    responseSchemaHash: digestInterruptJson(\n      canonicalInterruptJson(responseSchema),\n    ),\n  },\n)\n```\n\nThe client treats this as an untyped generic interrupt. The example above can\nstage a value, cancel it, or clear the draft. It can also join\n`resolveInterrupts(...)` with tool approvals and first-party generic interrupts.\n\n`v` is the binding wire version. The client rejects unknown versions and bad\nfields. Those interrupts become `unbound` rather than a form that cannot\nresume the owner.\n\n## What about client tools?\n\nA tool with a `.client()` implementation runs in the browser on its own and\nreports its own result. That is not a decision you make, so it never appears in\n`interrupts`. See [Client Tools](../tools/client-tools).\n\nIf persistence restores a pending client-tool execution, the client leaves it\npending rather than running the browser code again. See\n[Client persistence](../persistence/client-persistence#handle-restored-client-tools)\nfor recovery policies.\n\nThe one time a tool pauses is when you mark it `needsApproval: true`. Then it\nstops for a yes or no first, whether it runs on the server or in the browser:\n\n| Tool | What you handle |\n| --- | --- |\n| Server tool | Nothing, unless `needsApproval` adds a `tool-approval` pause. It then runs on the server after you approve. |\n| Client tool | Nothing, it runs in the browser automatically. With `needsApproval` it pauses for approval first, then runs in the browser. |\n\nSo approval is the only thing you resolve for either kind of tool, and both use\nthe same `tool-approval` interrupt.\n\n## Where to go next\n\n| You want to | Page |\n| --- | --- |\n| Approve or reject a single tool call | [Tool Approval](./tool-approval) |\n| Resolve several pending decisions at once | [Multiple Interrupts](./multiple) |\n| Ask the user something that isn't a tool | [Generic Interrupts](./generic) |\n| Read an MCP input request | [MCP client input](../tools/mcp-input) |\n| Pick `beforeModel`, `afterModel`, `beforeTools`, or `afterTools` | [Lifecycle Boundaries](./boundaries) |\n| Apply a generic answer to prompts or stop the run | [Apply Answers](./apply-answers) |\n| Run a tool in the browser | [Client Tools](../tools/client-tools) |\n| Move off the old `approval-requested` events | [Migration](./migration) |\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Overview\nid: interrupts-overview\norder: 1\ndescription: \"Pause an agent run for a human or application decision, then continue it exactly where it stopped.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - ag-ui interrupts\n  - human in the loop\n  - tool approval\n  - resolveInterrupt\n---\n\nMost agent runs are fire and forget. The model calls tools, they run, you get an\nanswer back. But some steps must not happen on their own: moving money,\ndeleting a project, sending an email. And sometimes the agent needs an answer\nonly the user can give before it can go on.\n\nAn interrupt is a pause. The run stops, hands you a decision to make, and then\npicks up exactly where it left off once you answer.\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The server reaches a step that needs a decision and ends the run with an\n   `interrupt` outcome instead of a final answer.\n2. The client gives you the pending decisions as `interrupts`.\n3. You resolve each one (approve, reject, submit a value, or cancel).\n4. The client starts a fresh continuation run that carries your answers and\n   continues the agent.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant User\n    participant Client\n    participant Server\n\n    Client->>Server: send message, run starts\n    Server-->>Client: interrupt outcome, run ends without a final answer\n    Client->>User: pending decisions surface as `interrupts`\n    User->>Client: approve / reject / submit a value\n    Client->>Server: continuation request with the answers, a fresh run\n    Server-->>Client: the agent picks up where it paused, final answer\n```\n\nNote that the pause spans **two runs**: the interrupted one ends, and the\ncontinuation is a new run. One user-visible turn, two run lifecycles. See\n[Threads and runs](../chat/stream-events#threads-and-runs).\n\nWhen replaying saved events, the client follows `parentRunId` links to avoid\nshowing a resolved pause again. A continuation that ends without an interrupt\nclears its ancestor's pending decisions, even if the parent link arrives later.\nIntermediate `tool_calls` events do not mark the run or its ancestors as answered.\n\nNo database is required. The browser sends the full message history back on the\ncontinuation request, so a stateless server can rebuild the paused step and keep\ngoing.\n\n## What pauses a run\n\nTwo kinds of interrupt show up in the `interrupts` array for you to resolve:\n\n| `kind` | You get a pause when | Guide |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `tool-approval` | A tool is marked `needsApproval` and the model calls it | [Tool Approval](./tool-approval) |\n| `generic` | Middleware requests typed client data at a lifecycle boundary | [Generic Interrupts](./generic) |\n\nAn MCP server can also ask for input during a tool call. The run ends with an interrupt. The page is [MCP client input](../tools/mcp-input).\n\n## First-party generic interrupts\n\nFor a generic interrupt that TanStack AI owns, define it once with\n`defineInterrupt()`. Register the definition with both `chat({ interrupts })`\nand `useChat({ interrupts })`. Middleware emits it through\n`onInterruptBoundary`, and the client receives a typed bound item that it can\nresolve or cancel. See [Generic Interrupts](./generic). To pick a phase, see\n[Lifecycle Boundaries](./boundaries). To apply the answer, see\n[Apply Answers](./apply-answers).\n\n## External generic interrupts\n\nAn interrupt is a standard AG-UI object, and TanStack AI is not the only thing\nthat can put one on a stream. A workflow engine pausing for a durable approval,\nor another agent framework sharing the same connection, emits the same envelope.\n\nThere are three cases:\n\n- A registered first-party generic interrupt has `kind: 'generic'`, a literal\n  `definitionId`, typed `payload`, and a typed `resolveInterrupt` method.\n- An external generic interrupt with a valid binding also has `kind: 'generic'`.\n  Its response is `unknown`, but it has `resolveInterrupt`, `cancel`, and\n  `clearResolution`. It joins the root batch controls.\n- An interrupt with a missing, malformed, or unsupported binding has\n  `kind: 'unbound'`. It remains visible, but has no controls.\n\nThe binding is stored in the interrupt metadata under\n`INTERRUPT_BINDING_METADATA_KEY`. It records the interrupted run and generation.\nThe client uses it to send the answer to the matching paused step.\n\nRender unbound items as status information. Do not render a response form for\nthem:\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst transferTool = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'transfer',\n  description: 'Move money between accounts',\n  needsApproval: true,\n  inputSchema: z.object({ recipient: z.string(), amount: z.number() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ receiptId: z.string() }),\n}).client()\n\nexport function Pauses() {\n  const { interrupts } = useChat({\n    threadId: 'thread-1',\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    tools: [transferTool] as const,\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      {interrupts.map((interrupt) => {\n        if (interrupt.kind === 'unbound') {\n          return (\n            <p key={interrupt.id}>\n              External pause: {interrupt.message ?? interrupt.reason}\n            </p>\n          )\n        }\n        if (interrupt.kind === 'generic') {\n          return (\n            <article key={interrupt.id}>\n              <p>{interrupt.message ?? interrupt.reason}</p>\n              <button\n                onClick={() =>\n                  interrupt.resolveInterrupt({ speed: 'express' })\n                }\n              >\n                Choose express\n              </button>\n              <button onClick={() => interrupt.cancel()}>Cancel</button>\n              <button onClick={() => interrupt.clearResolution()}>\n                Clear choice\n              </button>\n            </article>\n          )\n        }\n        return (\n          <button\n            key={interrupt.id}\n            onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}\n          >\n            Approve {interrupt.toolName}\n          </button>\n        )\n      })}\n    </>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe library will not invent a binding to make these resolvable. Doing so would\nrender a form whose answer is sent to a run that has nothing pending. The\nsubmit would fail only after the user has filled it in. `unbound` says that\nthe pause belongs to something else. Unbound items never block you from\nresolving the ones that are yours.\n\nIf an external producer wants the chat client to resume its pause, attach a\nvalid binding with `withInterruptBinding`. Do not write the metadata key by\nhand. Use the exact interrupted run id and generation that own the pause:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  INTERRUPT_BINDING_VERSION,\n  canonicalInterruptJson,\n  digestInterruptJson,\n  withInterruptBinding,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nconst responseSchema = {\n  type: 'object',\n  properties: { speed: { type: 'string' } },\n  required: ['speed'],\n}\n\nconst descriptor = withInterruptBinding(\n  {\n    id: 'shipping-1',\n    reason: 'confirmation',\n    message: 'Which shipping speed?',\n    responseSchema,\n  },\n  {\n    v: INTERRUPT_BINDING_VERSION,\n    kind: 'generic',\n    interruptId: 'shipping-1',\n    interruptedRunId: 'run-42',\n    generation: 0,\n    // The server checks the schema it hands out still matches the one it\n    // validates against, so the hash is computed from the schema itself.\n    responseSchemaHash: digestInterruptJson(\n      canonicalInterruptJson(responseSchema),\n    ),\n  },\n)\n```\n\nThe client treats this as an untyped generic interrupt. The example above can\nstage a value, cancel it, or clear the draft. It can also join\n`resolveInterrupts(...)` with tool approvals and first-party generic interrupts.\n\n`v` is the binding wire version. The client rejects unknown versions and bad\nfields. Those interrupts become `unbound` rather than a form that cannot\nresume the owner.\n\n## What about client tools?\n\nA tool with a `.client()` implementation runs in the browser on its own and\nreports its own result. That is not a decision you make, so it never appears in\n`interrupts`. See [Client Tools](../tools/client-tools).\n\nIf persistence restores a pending client-tool execution, the client leaves it\npending rather than running the browser code again. See\n[Client persistence](../persistence/client-persistence#handle-restored-client-tools)\nfor recovery policies.\n\nThe one time a tool pauses is when you mark it `needsApproval: true`. Then it\nstops for a yes or no first, whether it runs on the server or in the browser:\n\n| Tool | What you handle |\n| --- | --- |\n| Server tool | Nothing, unless `needsApproval` adds a `tool-approval` pause. It then runs on the server after you approve. |\n| Client tool | Nothing, it runs in the browser automatically. With `needsApproval` it pauses for approval first, then runs in the browser. |\n\nSo approval is the only thing you resolve for either kind of tool, and both use\nthe same `tool-approval` interrupt.\n\n## Where to go next\n\n| You want to | Page |\n| --- | --- |\n| Approve or reject a single tool call | [Tool Approval](./tool-approval) |\n| Resolve several pending decisions at once | [Multiple Interrupts](./multiple) |\n| Ask the user something that isn't a tool | [Generic Interrupts](./generic) |\n| Read an MCP input request | [MCP client input](../tools/mcp-input) |\n| Pick `beforeModel`, `afterModel`, `beforeTools`, or `afterTools` | [Lifecycle Boundaries](./boundaries) |\n| Apply a generic answer to prompts or stop the run | [Apply Answers](./apply-answers) |\n| Run a tool in the browser | [Client Tools](../tools/client-tools) |\n| Move off the old `approval-requested` events | [Migration](./migration) |\n"}
